rs and lifts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1997</span><span class="rf-text">Milošević, his last legal term as President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> over, becomes President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1998</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> War begins between Yugoslav forces and the Kosovo Liberation Army</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1999</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> bombs <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, and Security Council Resolution 1244 places <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> mission</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2000</span><span class="rf-text">The Bulldozer Revolution removes Milošević, and the country joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> as a new member</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2003</span><span class="rf-text">The Constitutional Charter turns the federation into the State Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>During the collapse of the Socialist Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and the Serb rebel-held territories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> agreed on 26 December 1991 to form a new “third Yugoslavia”. Efforts in 1991 to bring the Socialist Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> into the federation, through talks between Milošević, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>'s Serbian Democratic Party and the Bosniak vice-president Adil Zulfikarpašić, ended by late 1991, when Izetbegović planned a referendum on independence and violence broke out between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosniaks">Bosniaks</a>.</p><p>The Federal Republic of Yugoslavia was established on 27 April 1992 as a federation of the Republic of Serbia and the Republic of Montenegro, essentially a rump state with a Serb majority. It claimed to continue the international legal personality and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> membership of the old federation, but Security Council Resolution 777 held that the Socialist Federal Republic had ceased to exist, and General Assembly Resolution 47/1 barred the new state from the Assembly's work. The new state abandoned the communist legacy: the red star was removed from the flag, and a coat of arms combining Serbian and Montenegrin elements replaced the communist one.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The 1992 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 10,394,026 inhabitants: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> 62.6%, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> 16.5% and Montenegrins 5%, with Hungarians, Yugoslavs, Muslims, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> and others. Most of this diversity lay in the autonomous provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a>, and the Albanian population was concentrated in Kosovo. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, with a metropolitan population of 1,574,050, was the only city of significant size. The country covered 102,350 square kilometres, with 199 kilometres of coastline, all of it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">Sanctions</a> imposed during the Yugoslav Wars brought economic collapse. Fuel stations ran out of petrol, foreign assets were seized and average income was halved from $3,000 to $1,500; an estimated 3 million people lived below the poverty line and thousands of young citizens emigrated. From 1992 to 1994 the dinar suffered hyperinflation reaching 313 million percent, and much of the country, including all of Montenegro, used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deutsche-mark">Deutsche Mark</a> instead. After the Kosovo War the economy was only half its 1990 size. Corruption, a large black market and severe unemployment persisted after 2000, when the new government began market reform and renewed its membership of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">International Monetary Fund</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> (Vojska Jugoslavije, VJ) was formed from the remnants of the Yugoslav People's Army (JNA), with ground, naval, air and air defence forces and civil defence. From 1992 to 1995 the government supported the Croatian and Bosnian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, transferring units of the old JNA to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-serbian-krajina">Republic of Serbian Krajina</a> and the Republika Srpska rather than fighting openly. Its own role was then limited, apart from conflicts on the Croatian border such as the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> left its units without oil or munitions.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> the Kosovo Liberation Army began attacking Serbian police in 1996, and Milošević sent Yugoslav Army units against the insurgency; open war followed in 1998. Attacks were often directed against civilians, and 863,000 Albanian civilians were forcibly expelled between March and June 1999, and of 10,317 civilians killed or missing from 1998 to 2000, 8,676 were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a>; some 169,824 Serb and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a> civilians fled the province. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> answered in March 1999 with airstrikes on military targets and infrastructure, without the Security Council's approval. Resolution 1244 forced the withdrawal of Yugoslav forces from Kosovo.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Federal Assembly had two chambers: the Council of Citizens, elected by the people, and the Council of Republics, with equal representation for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>. The president, a figurehead elected by the Assembly, could be dismissed for violating the constitution, while the prime minister ran the government. The federation joined the Republic of Serbia, with its autonomous provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metohija">Metohija</a>, and the Republic of Montenegro under a cooperative federalism.</p><p>Real power lay with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slobodan-milosevic">Slobodan Milošević</a> and his Socialist Party of Serbia, who ran both republics as an effective <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>. The first president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobrica-cosic">Dobrica Ćosić</a> (1992–1993), was forced out for opposing him. The federal prime minister Milan Panić, who once told Milošević to “shut up”, was made to resign. Zoran Lilić served until 1997, when Milošević, his last legal term as President of Serbia over, had himself elected federal president; after the disputed election of 2000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojislav-kostunica">Vojislav Koštunica</a> held the office until 2003. In April 1999 the Federal Assembly voted to join the Union State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, a decision never acted on.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The FR <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> shaped the Yugoslav Wars through its backing of the Serb breakaway states, which it sought to establish as independent Serbian republics with a possible later reintegration. In 1995, after the Croatian Army's Operation Storm and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>'s involvement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnian-war">Bosnian War</a>, Milošević took over the negotiating powers of all the Serb secessionist movements. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayton-agreement">Dayton Agreement</a> recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> as sovereign states and gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> institutions within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> War left Kosovo an autonomous province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in name but under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> mission, politically and economically separate. The government estimated $100 billion in damage to infrastructure, and economists at least $29 billion in direct damage from the bombing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">Sanctions</a> also cut the country out of sport: it missed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1992-summer-olympics">1992 Summer Olympics</a> and was excluded from international football from 1992 to 1996, though its basketball team won the EuroBasket in 1995, 1997 and 2001 and the world titles of 1998 and 2002. FIFA and UEFA treated it as the only successor of Yugoslavia.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Military defeats and the collapse of the economy made Milošević and the Socialist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> deeply unpopular. In September 2000, amid accusations of electoral fraud, mass protests broke out, and in the Bulldozer Revolution his government was replaced by the Democratic Opposition of Serbia under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojislav-kostunica">Vojislav Koštunica</a>. The new government gave up the claim to be the sole successor of the Socialist Federal Republic and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> as a new member on 1 November 2000. Milošević was later tried at the International Criminal Tribunal for the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> but died in prison in 2006 before his trial ended.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> had already separated its economy from Serbia's in the Milošević era, with its own central bank and currency. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> Agreement of March 2002 led to the Constitutional Charter of 4 February 2003, which turned the federation into the State Union of Serbia and Montenegro and retired the name Yugoslavia after 74 years. The charter allowed a referendum on independence after three years: on 21 May 2006, 55.5% of Montenegrin voters chose independence, and Montenegro and Serbia declared it on 3 and 5 June 2006.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> from 1992 to 2003 after the old federation broke up. Dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slobodan-milosevic">Slobodan Milošević</a>, it backed the Serb breakaway states in the Yugoslav Wars, suffered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> and hyperinflation and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> War, before the Bulldozer Revolution of 2000 and its reconstitution as a state union in 2003.</em></p>

